    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Diffuse intrinsic pontine gliomas (DIPG) are highly aggressive tumors originating in the pons of the brainstem during childhood. With an average survival post-diagnosis of only 9 months, DIPG is the leading cause of pediatric brain tumor death. Because the unique biology underlying the disease is not well understood, little progress has been achieved in treating DIPG in the past thirty years. Here, we demonstrate that DIPG cells secrete soluble factors that promote abnormal proliferation of surrounding neural precursor cells. Using a combination of biochemical techniques, we identified SPARCL1 as a protein secreted by DIPG cells in vitro that is sufficient to increase both NPC and DIPG growth. Currently, therapeutic interventions mainly focus on targeting cancer cells themselves. However, we postulate that DIPG cells incorporate healthy neural precursor cells into the tumor mass. Thus, targeting interactions between DIPG cells and NPCs in the developing brain holds the potential to slow DIPG progression.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
